From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shipwright <shipwright@invalid>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 07:40:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Pause media when a bud leaves the ear and resume it when it
 is back

AirPods report ear detection over AAP (packet 0x06: the primary and the
secondary bud, each in the ear, out of it or in the case) but, unlike with
an iPhone, nothing on the host acts on it. Add:

- the read-only earDetection capability: primary, secondary and, once the
  battery packet has said which bud is primary, left and right;
- playback control over MPRIS on the session bus: when a worn bud is
  taken out, the player whose PlaybackStatus is Playing is paused and
  remembered; when the buds are back, that same player is resumed if it
  is still paused. A player the user plays or stops in the meantime is
  forgotten, so something the user paused is never resumed. Losing the
  link forgets it too;
- the earDetectionPause setting in the magicpods container: "off",
  "oneRemoved" (the default) or "bothRemoved".

The state machine (EarPauseController) is separate from D-Bus
(MprisClient). MprisClient uses one connection without an event loop
thread: the players' PropertiesChanged signals wait in its queue and are
delivered in order when the controller asks, and by Pause and Play up to
the player's answer, so a report sent before the player answered our
Pause is never mistaken for the user taking over.

DEBUG self-tests in TestsAapEarDetection cover the parser and the state
machine with fake players. TestsEarPauseMpris runs the controller with
the real MprisClient against fake players on a private bus, when
MAGICPODS_TEST_MPRIS is set.

Protocol reference: LibrePods (https://github.com/librepods-org/librepods),
"AAP Definitions.md", "Ear Detection", and its Linux client's ear
detection and media controller (pause when one bud is removed by
default).
